{
 "lead": "Speech and data sit near 0 Hz, so a wire or antenna will not carry them far. Modulation lifts the message onto a high-frequency sine carrier by varying its amplitude, frequency or phase. The same three knobs carry bits, giving ASK, FSK and PSK. This chapter covers AM, FM and PM, then the digital forms up to QAM, where baud rate and bit rate differ.",
 "prereq": "You need <a href=\"#analog-and-digital-signals\">2.1</a> for amplitude, frequency and phase, <a href=\"#data-rate-limits-nyquist-and-shannon-capacity\">2.3</a> for bandwidth and capacity, and <a href=\"#guided-and-unguided-transmission-media\">2.4</a> for band-pass media.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Why a signal needs a carrier",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A baseband signal — voice or bits — holds most energy near 0 Hz. Many media do not pass 0 Hz. A radio channel, cable TV plant or telephone line with a transformer passes only a band around a centre frequency. That is a band-pass channel. Baseband pushed into it gives nothing useful out."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Modulation fixes this. The carrier is the sine wave A<sub>c</sub> cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t), where A<sub>c</sub> is carrier peak amplitude in volts, f<sub>c</sub> carrier frequency in hertz, and t time in seconds. Vary one carrier parameter in step with the message. The message now rides inside the channel band."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The antenna is a second reason. An antenna radiates well near a quarter wavelength, λ/4, with λ = c/f and c = 3 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s. A 1 kHz tone has λ = 300 km, so λ/4 = 75 km — useless. At 100 MHz, λ = 3 m and λ/4 = 75 cm. Higher frequency makes radiation practical."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Third, many signals can share one medium on different carriers. That is frequency-division multiplexing (FDM); <a href=\"#multiplexing-fdm-tdm-and-wdm\">2.7</a> builds on it."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Amplitude modulation",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In AM the carrier amplitude follows the message. For one tone:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "s(t) = A<sub>c</sub> [1 + μ cos(2πf<sub>m</sub>t)] cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t)",
     "where": "A<sub>c</sub> is carrier peak amplitude in V, f<sub>c</sub> carrier frequency in Hz, f<sub>m</sub> message frequency in Hz, t time in s, and μ = A<sub>m</sub>/A<sub>c</sub> the modulation index (no units), with A<sub>m</sub> message peak amplitude in V."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The square bracket is the envelope. It swings between A<sub>c</sub>(1 + μ) and A<sub>c</sub>(1 − μ). Multiplying the cosines gives the carrier f<sub>c</sub> and two sidebands at f<sub>c</sub> − f<sub>m</sub> and f<sub>c</sub> + f<sub>m</sub>. One tone occupies 2f<sub>m</sub> Hz. A message band reaching f<sub>m(max)</sub> takes 2f<sub>m(max)</sub> Hz."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Keep μ ≤ 1. At μ = 1 the envelope just touches zero. Past that — overmodulation — the envelope crosses over, phase flips for part of each cycle, and recovered audio inverts and distorts. More depth is not more loudness; it is a broken signal."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The carrier alone carries no information. DSB-SC (double-sideband suppressed carrier) removes it to save power. SSB-SC (single-sideband suppressed carrier) also removes one sideband, halving bandwidth to f<sub>m</sub>; shortwave radio uses it."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Frequency and phase modulation",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In FM the instantaneous frequency is f<sub>c</sub> + k<sub>f</sub>m(t), where k<sub>f</sub> is frequency sensitivity in Hz/V and m(t) the message in volts. For a tone of peak amplitude A<sub>m</sub>, peak frequency deviation is Δf = k<sub>f</sub>A<sub>m</sub> Hz. The FM modulation index is β = Δf/f<sub>m</sub>, a pure number."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "B ≈ 2(Δf + f<sub>m</sub>)",
     "where": "B is practical FM bandwidth in Hz, Δf peak frequency deviation in Hz, and f<sub>m</sub> the highest message frequency in Hz (Carson's rule)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In PM the phase shifts in proportion to m(t); the index is peak phase deviation in radians. FM and PM waves have a constant envelope, so a limiter strips amplitude noise before detection — that is their noise immunity. The price is bandwidth: with β = 5, Carson gives B ≈ 12f<sub>m</sub>."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Digital modulation: ASK, FSK, PSK, QAM",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Digital modulation uses the same three knobs, driven by bits."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "ASK switches carrier amplitude between levels. When one level is zero it is on-off keying (OOK). Noise adds to amplitude, so ASK is fragile but cheap. A light source switched fully on and off is an ASK signal, which suits optical fibre."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "FSK jumps the carrier between two frequencies (BFSK). The envelope stays constant, so clipping and amplitude distortion do not matter; bandwidth is wider than ASK."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "PSK switches phase. BPSK uses two phases 180° apart, giving 1 bit per symbol. These symbols are as far apart as two points on a circle can be, so noise must be large to cause an error; of ASK, FSK and PSK, BPSK gives the fewest bit errors at the same signal-to-noise ratio. QPSK uses four phases 90° apart, giving 2 bits per symbol in the same bandwidth."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "QAM varies amplitude and phase together, placing M points in a constellation — a plot of the two carrier components. QAM-16 is a 4 × 4 grid with 4 bits per symbol; QAM-64 is an 8 × 8 grid with 6 bits per symbol. More points mean closer neighbours, so higher QAM needs a cleaner channel (see <a href=\"#transmission-impairments-attenuation-distortion-and-noise\">2.2</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "R<sub>b</sub> = R<sub>s</sub> × log₂M",
     "where": "R<sub>b</sub> is bit rate in bit/s, R<sub>s</sub> symbol (baud) rate in symbols/s, and M the number of constellation states."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Scheme",
      "M",
      "Bits per symbol"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "ASK, BFSK, BPSK",
       "2",
       "1"
      ],
      [
       "QPSK",
       "4",
       "2"
      ],
      [
       "QAM-16",
       "16",
       "4"
      ],
      [
       "QAM-64",
       "64",
       "6"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The modem does this at both ends: modulator on send, demodulator on receive."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
