 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A 1 kHz tone with peak amplitude 6 V amplitude-modulates a 100 kHz carrier of peak amplitude 10 V. Find μ, sidebands, bandwidth and peak amplitude. A QAM-16 modem runs at 2400 baud; find its bit rate.",
   "steps": [
    "Data: f<sub>m</sub> = 1 kHz, A<sub>m</sub> = 6 V, f<sub>c</sub> = 100 kHz, A<sub>c</sub> = 10 V.",
    "μ = A<sub>m</sub>/A<sub>c</sub> = 6 ÷ 10 = 0.6, so 60% and no overmodulation.",
    "Lower sideband: f<sub>c</sub> − f<sub>m</sub> = 100 − 1 = 99 kHz.",
    "Upper sideband: f<sub>c</sub> + f<sub>m</sub> = 100 + 1 = 101 kHz.",
    "Bandwidth: 101 − 99 = 2 kHz = 2f<sub>m</sub>.",
    "Peak amplitude: A<sub>c</sub>(1 + μ) = 10 × 1.6 = 16 V; minimum = 10 × 0.4 = 4 V.",
    "QAM-16 carries log₂16 = 4 bits per symbol.",
    "Bit rate: 2400 × 4 = 9600 bit/s."
   ],
   "answer": "μ = 0.6 (60%) with no overmodulation, sidebands at 99 kHz and 101 kHz, bandwidth 2 kHz, peak amplitude 16 V (minimum 4 V), and the QAM-16 modem delivers 4 bits per symbol at 9600 bit/s.",
   "check": "import math\nAm = 6.0\nAc = 10.0\nfm = 1e3\nfc = 100e3\nmu = Am/Ac\nprint(mu)\nprint((fc-fm)/1e3)\nprint((fc+fm)/1e3)\nprint(2*fm/1e3)\nprint(Ac*(1+mu))\nprint(Ac*(1-mu))\nM = 16\nbps = math.log2(M)\nprint(bps)\nprint(2400*bps)\n",
   "values": [
    0.6,
    99.0,
    101.0,
    2.0,
    16.0,
    4.0,
    4.0,
    9600.0
   ],
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "question": "A QAM-64 modem runs at 2000 baud over a clean link. Find its bit rate.",
   "steps": [
    "States: M = 64.",
    "Bits per symbol = log₂64 = 6, since 2⁶ = 64.",
    "Bit rate = R<sub>s</sub> × log₂M = 2000 × 6 = 12000 bit/s."
   ],
   "answer": "The bit rate is 12000 bit/s, i.e. 12 kbit/s.",
   "check": "import math\nRs = 2000\nM = 64\nbps = math.log2(M)\nprint(bps)\nprint(Rs*bps)\n",
   "values": [
    6.0,
    12000.0
   ],
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "μ > 1 is not deeper modulation. Past 100% the envelope crosses over and audio inverts and distorts.",
  "Bit rate is baud rate × log₂M, not baud rate × M. QAM-16 at 2400 baud is 9600 bit/s, not 38400 bit/s.",
  "A single-tone AM wave has bandwidth 2f<sub>m</sub>, not f<sub>m</sub>. The f<sub>m</sub> figure belongs to single sideband.",
  "In FM, Δf is peak frequency deviation, not bandwidth. Carson's rule adds Δf and f<sub>m</sub> before doubling.",
  "Do not swap f<sub>c</sub> and f<sub>m</sub>: sidebands are f<sub>c</sub> ± f<sub>m</sub>, so the carrier is the larger number in an AM radio link."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define the AM modulation index and state the condition for no overmodulation.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "μ = A<sub>m</sub>/A<sub>c</sub>, message peak amplitude divided by carrier peak amplitude. No overmodulation when μ ≤ 1."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does the carrier of an AM wave carry no information?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "It is a fixed sine at f<sub>c</sub> with constant amplitude; all message-dependent variation is in the two sidebands. Removing it — DSB-SC — leaves the message recoverable."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does FM resist noise better than AM, and what does it pay?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "FM keeps a constant envelope, so a limiter removes amplitude noise before detection. It pays in bandwidth: Carson gives B ≈ 2(Δf + f<sub>m</sub>), many times the message bandwidth."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A 2 kHz tone with peak amplitude 4 V modulates a 200 kHz carrier of peak amplitude 8 V. Find μ, sidebands, bandwidth and peak amplitude.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "μ = 4 ÷ 8 = 0.5. Sidebands at 198 kHz and 202 kHz. Bandwidth = 4 kHz = 2f<sub>m</sub>. Peak amplitude = 8 × 1.5 = 12 V."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A QAM-16 modem transmits 3000 symbols per second. Find its bit rate.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "log₂16 = 4 bits per symbol, so bit rate = 3000 × 4 = 12000 bit/s."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "An operator drives an AM transmitter to μ = 1.4 to get more power across. Explain what a listener hears.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "The envelope passes through zero and crosses over for part of each cycle, so phase inverts. The envelope detector folds the waveform and recovered audio distorts and partly inverts. Overmodulation causes splatter into neighbouring channels, not more loudness."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Why does QAM-64 need a cleaner channel than QAM-16 at the same peak amplitude?",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "QAM-64 puts 64 points into the same peak swing, so neighbours are closer than in the 16-point grid. The same noise voltage is more likely to cross a decision boundary, so a higher signal-to-noise ratio is needed."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Wi-Fi uses QAM-64 up to QAM-1024 with OFDM, and LTE goes to 256-QAM. Error-vector magnitude (EVM) measures how far received symbols sit from ideal points. Software-defined radios change scheme in code. Search constellation diagram, EVM and peak-to-average power ratio (PAPR)."
